ta"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right w-4 h-4 text-muted-foreground group-hover:text-orange-400 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-all","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]}],["$","li","/compare/stoicism-vs-vedanta",{"children":["$","$L18",null,{"href":"/compare/stoicism-vs-vedanta","className":"flex items-center justify-between p-3 rounded-xl hover:bg-white/5 transition-colors group","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium group-hover:text-orange-400 transition-colors","children":"Stoicism vs Vedanta"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right w-4 h-4 text-muted-foreground group-hover:text-orange-400 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-all","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]}],["$","li","/learn/sanskrit/brahman",{"children":["$","$L18",null,{"href":"/learn/sanskrit/brahman","className":"flex items-center justify-between p-3 rounded-xl hover:bg-white/5 transition-colors group","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium group-hover:text-orange-400 transition-colors","children":"What is Brahman?"}],["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right w-4 h-4 text-muted-foreground group-hover:text-orange-400 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-all","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]}]]}]]}]
38:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-16","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-8","children":"Frequently Asked Questions"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"What is non-duality?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Non-duality (Advaita) is the philosophical position that ultimate reality is singular and undivided. All apparent distinctions — self/other, subject/object, individual/universe — are superimpositions on a single underlying consciousness."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"What is dualism in Indian philosophy?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Dualism (Dvaita) holds that two categories of reality are eternally distinct — typically conscious souls (Purusha) and inert matter (Prakriti) in Samkhya, or God and individual souls in Madhva's Vedanta."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"Which is more popular — non-duality or dualism?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Both have enormous followings. Advaita Vedanta is the most internationally influential Indian philosophy. Dvaita has deep roots in South India and strongly influences the Bhakti movement. Both traditions have living practitioners in the millions."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"Can you practice spirituality without taking a position?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Yes. Many practitioners of bhakti (devotion) remain effectively in a dualistic relationship with God their whole lives and reach liberation. The Bhagavad Gita itself says the paths of Jnana and Bhakti both lead to the same liberation."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"How does Vishishtadvaita differ from both?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Vishishtadvaita ('qualified non-duality') accepts that reality is ultimately one (Brahman) but insists that souls and matter are real components within that oneness — like cells within a body. It occupies the middle ground."}]]}],["$","div","5",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"Is non-duality just another word for monism?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Not exactly. Monism says 'there is only one substance.' Non-duality is more subtle — it says that the distinction between observer and observed, between 'this' and 'that,' is not ultimately real. Some non-dual traditions reject even the concept of 'one thing.'"}]]}],["$","div","6",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"Does non-duality make ethics meaningless?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"No. Non-dual teachers are consistent on this: at the relative (Vyavaharika) level, cause and effect, ethics, and relationships are fully real. Liberation doesn't dissolve ethics — it often deepens compassion by removing selfish motivation."}]]}],"$L3d"]}]]}]
39:["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gradient-to-br from-orange-950/30 to-background rounded-3xl border border-orange-900/30 p-10 text-center","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-display font-bold mb-4","children":"Ready to find your path?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-muted-foreground mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto","children":"Take the Faith Finder — a 5-minute quiz that maps your temperament to the specific tradition, practice, and philosophy within Sanatan Dharma that fits you."}],["$","$L18",null,{"href":"/faith-finder","children":["$","button",null,{"className":"inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 whitespace-nowrap font-medium ring-offset-background focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 disabled:pointer-events-none disabled:opacity-50 [&_svg]:pointer-events-none [&_svg]:size-4 [&_svg]:shrink-0 bg-orange-500 text-white hover:bg-orange-600 h-14 px-8 rounded-full text-lg shadow-lg shadow-orange-900/20 transition-transform hover:scale-105","ref":"$undefined","children":["Start the Faith Finder",["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right ml-2 w-5 h-5","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]}]]}]
3d:["$","div","7",{"className":"p-6 rounded-2xl border border-border/50 bg-card","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-lg mb-3","children":"What Western philosophy is closest to Advaita?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed","children":"Spinoza's pantheism, Hegel's Absolute Idealism, and aspects of Neoplatonism are frequently compared. Schopenhauer explicitly studied Upanishadic thought. However, Indian non-duality includes specific meditative practices absent from Western counterparts."}]]}]
